===Verb===
{{en-verb|es}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[give#Verb|give]]; especially, to give as a [[gift#Noun|gift]] during a [[#Noun|potlatch]] [[ceremony]].
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=[Accompanying Papers. Alaska.] Appendix M. Letter from William S. Dodge, Ex-mayor of Sitka, on Affairs in Alaska Generally.|journal=Report of the Commissioner of Indian Affairs, Made to the Secretary of the Interior, for the Year 1869|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|Government Printing Office]]|date=10 November 1869|year_published=1870|page=589|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Ox4BbF_TcaUC&pg=PA589|oclc=20180185|passage=He was a Chilkaht chief, and it being New Year's day, he had been to General Davis's house and "'''potlatched'''" (treated) to a bottle of whiskey.|brackets=on}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=About the Shores of Puget Sound|magazine=[[w:Overland Monthly|The Overland Monthly: Devoted to the Development of the Country]]|location=San Francisco, Calif.|publisher=John H. Carmany & Company|month=September|year=1871|volume=VII|issue=3|page=282|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=CqBHAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA282|column=2|oclc=1011856346|passage=I came to see if you can '''''potlatch''''' a old sword, and ''hi-yu buttons'' [plenty buttons].}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=I[srael] W[ood] Powell|authorlink=Israel Wood Powell|title=[Report on Indian Affairs in the Province of British Columbia]|journal=Dominion of Canada: Annual Report of the Department of Indian Affairs for the Year Ended 31st December, 1885.{{nb...|Printed by Order of Parliament.}}|location=Ottawa|publisher={{...|Printed by}} Maclean, Roger & Co.,{{nb...|Wellington Street.}}|date=2 November 1885|year_published=1886|page=120|pageurl=https://babel.hathitrust.org/cgi/pt?id=uc1.$b536197&view=1up&seq=188|oclc=899258994|passage=The chief was permitted to meet his many creditors and return the goods he had received at previous feasts on the distinct understanding, however, that no gifts were to be "'''potlached'''" ''i.e.'', donated with the usual custom of having them returned.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=F. M. Kelly|title=Over the Backbone of Vancouver Island|editor=Frank H. Mayer|magazine=Western Field: The Sportsman’s Magazine of the West|location=San Francisco, Calif.|publisher=The Western Field Company|month=February|year=1908|volume=12|issue=1|page=37|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/westernfield121908olym/page/37/mode/1up|column=1|oclc=5139096|passage=After giving them a feed and '''potlaching''' some grub for their back trip, we paid the Indians off, after making arrangements with them to return at a certain date.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Edward Sapir|authorlink=Edward Sapir|author2=Morris Swadesh|authorlink2=Morris Swadesh|title=Native Accounts of Nootka Ethnography|location=Bloomington, Ind.|publisher=Center in Anthropology, Folklore and Linguistics, {{w|Indiana University}}|year=1955|page=263|oclc=10999848|passage=Then he gave out half dollars to all the Huupachas men and women because Kwalats (Gaillic's oldest boy) had stumbled against the saw he was going to '''potlatch'''. Charly Ross finished '''potlatching'''. The Tishaa were agog over the potlatching for the child who had stumbled.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=James Kari|authorlink=James Kari|chapter=Alexander Creek. [Ghuliy—Potlatch Wealth.]|mainauthor=James Kari; James A. Fall; Shem Pete|title=Shem Pete’s Alaska: The Territory of the Upper Cook Inlet Dena’ina|edition=2nd|location=Fairbanks, Ak.|publisher={{w|University of Alaska Press}}|year=2003|page=116|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=y9oUBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA116|column=1|isbn=978-1-889963-56-3|passage=When Big Chilligan died in 1931, the shirt was '''potlatched''' to Simeon Chickalusion, the chief at Tyonek. Upon Chickalusion's death in 1957, the shirt was '''potlatched''' to Shem Pete.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Paul Helliwell|title=Zombie Nation|editor=Josephine Berry Slater|magazine=[[w:Mute (magazine)|Mute]]|location=London|publisher=Pauline van Mourik Broekman; Simon Worthington|month=April|year=2007|volume=2|issue=5|page=79|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=x6jfV-dmHIoC&pg=PA79|column=1|isbn=978-0-9554796-1-8|issn=1356-7748|passage=The surplus of recorded sound on the computers of the world cannot be '''potlatched''' because to make a digital copy does not destroy the original or reduce its value. Music cannot even be given away because nothing is lost, it can only be shared in the weakest sense of the term.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[carry out]] or [[take part]] in a potlatch ceremony.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=John Evans, witness|title=To Prohibit Fishing for Salmon in the Yukon River. Part 2: Hearings before the {{w|United States House Committee on Merchant Marine and Fisheries}}, Subcommittee on Fish and Fish Hatcheries, Sixty-Sixth [[w:United States Congress|Congress]], Second Session, on May 4, 1920|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|U.S. Government Printing Office]]|date=4 May 1920|page=20|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=9SPN3c88J88C&pg=PA23-IA7|oclc=968581646|passage=Natives did not get their wheels into the water until the [salmon] run was nearly over; were all '''potlaching''' until the latter part of July.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Robert A. McKennan|title=The Upper Tanana Indians|series=Yale Publications in Anthropology|seriesvolume=no. 55|location=New Haven, Conn.|publisher=Department of Anthropology, {{w|Yale University}}|year=1959|page=118|oclc=1108889576|newversion=quoted in|2ndauthor={{w|Marie-Françoise Guédon}}|chapter2=Life Cycle|title2=People of Tetlin, Why are You Singing?|series2=Mercury Series, Ethnology Division Paper|seriesvolume2=no. 9|location2=Ottawa|publisher2=[[w:Canadian Museum of History|National Museum of Man]], [[w:National museums of Canada|National Museums of Canada]]|year2=1974|page2=186|pageurl2=https://archive.org/details/peopleoftetlinwh0000gued/page/186/mode/1up|oclc2=465551181|passage=While girls are married as soon as they mature, the men marry much later, for custom dictates that a man to be eligible for marriage must have '''potlached''' at least once and preferably three times. This rule is gradually breaking down, but even now public opinion frowns on early marriage by young men.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Philip Drucker|authorlink=Philip Drucker|author2=Robert F[leming] Heizer|authorlink2=Robert Heizer|chapter=The Verbalization of Conflict in the Potlatch|title=To Make My Name Good: A Reexamination of the Southern Kwakiutl Potlatch|location=Berkeley; Los Angeles, Calif.; London|publisher={{w|University of California Press}}|year=1967|page=134|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=yJy949qBeoEC&pg=PA134|oclc=601733220|passage=The misconception so often encountered in anthropological literature that an Indian gained social status by '''potlatching''', or '''potlatched''' to gain social status, comes in part from the Indians themselves; it is thus one of the categories of ethnographic fact mentioned in earlier pages, what people say they do. [...] Close analysis reveals that the reverse was actually true: So-and-so gave many (read "several major") potlatches because he was a great ("highly ranked") chief. [...] The only exceptions to this rule, and they were few indeed, were those who in relatively recent times, by '''potlatching''' and by otherwise establishing good personal relationships with the chiefs, were able to persuade the chiefs to create eagle places for them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Harumi Befu|chapter=Structural and Motivational Approaches to Social Exchange|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=F8i5BgAAQBAJ&pg=PT243|editors=Kenneth J. Gergen, Martin S. Greenberg, and Richard H. Willis|title=Social Exchange: Advances in Theory and Research|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Plenum Press, {{w|Springer Science+Business Media}}|year=1980|year_published=2012|doi=10.1007/978-1-4613-3087-5_9|isbn=978-1-4613-3089-9|passage=When they describe, for example, payment of cattle for a bride in East Africa, offering a gift in India to earn spiritual merit, exchanging favors between compadres in Middle America, giving a ceremonial necklace in return for an arm shell in the Trobriand kula ring, and '''potlaching''' each other by Kwakiutl chiefs, ethnographers are assuming the operation of some reciprocal principle.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Harry Assu|author2=with Joy Inglis|chapter=|title=Assu of Cape Mudge: Recollections of a Coastal Indian Chief|location=Vancouver, B.C.|publisher={{w|University of British Columbia Press}}|year=1989|page=107|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=20LmlBUCPMQC&pg=PA107|isbn=978-0-7748-0333-5|passage=My boys and I put up a totem pole to my father inside the museum. [...] When you raise a pole you have to '''potlatch'''. I '''potlatched''' for all the tribes when they gathered here for the museum opening.}}

